<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glastonbury Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glastonbury Abbey was a Benedictine monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, founded by the seventh century and patronised by the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kings. Reformed by Dunstan in the tenth century, it became one of the richest abbeys in England and was linked in legend with Joseph of Arimathea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a>, whose supposed grave the monks found in 1191. It was dissolved in 1539.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey.jpg" alt="Glastonbury Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Slater · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glastonbury_Abbey_church_view_from_east.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations have shown Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> occupation of the site, and the abbey was founded by Britons. Saxons under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenwalh-of-wessex">Cenwalh of Wessex</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> as far as the River Parrett after the Battle of Peonnum in 658 and let the British abbot Bregored stay in power. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a> enriched the community and is reputed to have ordered a stone church in 712. In the 10th century Dunstan enlarged the church, built the cloisters and instituted the Benedictine Rule. Kings Edmund I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a> were buried there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> the abbey was the richest monastery in the country when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> was commissioned in 1086.</p><p>A great fire in 1184 destroyed the monastic buildings, and the Lady Chapel was consecrated in 1186. In 1191 the monks reported finding the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a> and Queen Guinevere. Giraldus Cambrensis wrote that a hollowed oak trunk with two skeletons lay about 16 feet down, beneath a lead cross naming Arthur on the Isle of Avalon. Historians today generally dismiss the find as a publicity stunt to raise funds for rebuilding. In the 14th century only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> was more richly endowed. The abbey also helped to drain the Somerset Levels, reclaiming 722 acres near Westonzoyland in 1234.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The abbey controlled large tracts of land and a water-borne transport network that grew from a canal of the mid-10th century to the River Brue. Stone came from its quarries at Doulting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> barns held crops at Doulting, Mells and Pilton. Fish were an important product, as the abbey needed them on Fridays, fast days and in Lent, and a Fish House built at Meare in the 14th century survives. Its library held unique early histories, and around 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are known to have survived the Dissolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In September 1539 Richard Layton, Richard Pollard and Thomas Moyle arrived without warning on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell">Thomas Cromwell</a>'s orders. The abbey was stripped of its valuables, and the last abbot, Richard Whiting, who had signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy</a>, resisted and was hanged, drawn and quartered as a traitor on Glastonbury Tor on 15 November 1539. Stone and lead were later taken from the ruins, and the Abbot's Kitchen is the only building to survive intact. The Ancient Monuments Protection Act 1882 stopped further damage.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and Wells Diocesan Trust bought the ruins in 1908. The great church and the Lady Chapel are grade I listed, and services in Anglican, Roman Catholic and Eastern Orthodox traditions are held there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glastonbury Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries such as Malmesbury and <b>Glastonbury</b> became centres of learning, and Boniface, a West Saxon monk probably born in Devon, worked to convert and organise the church in Germany in the eighth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
